    {
      "Id": "62c611f8-e447-4810-8c10-a7b454fd52ec",
      "Title": "APC40 Mk1 Feedback Example\n\nTixl Examples symbol: Examples.Lib.io.midi.APC40Mk1FeedbackExample\n\nSource:\nOperators/examples/Symbols/lib/io/midi/APC40Mk1FeedbackExample.t3\n\nAPC40 Mk1 modes and SysEx\n\nThe APC40 Mk1 compatible-device handler sends the introduction SysEx\nautomatically after it detects matching Akai APC40 input and output\nports. No separate SysEx checkbox is required. The Generic\ninitialization message is F0 47 7F 73 60 00 04 40 08 01 01 F7.\n\n- Shift + Record Arm 1 selects Generic mode (0x40), which lets this\ngraph's button MIDI pass through.\n- Shift + Record Arm 2 selects Ableton Live mode (0x41).\n- Shift + Record Arm 3 selects Preset/Snapshot mode (0x42, Akai's\nAlternate Live mode); the compatible handler consumes its snapshot\ncontrols.\n\nEncoder examples\n\n- Track Control knob 1: input CC 48; ring-style CC 56; Single style (1).\n- Track Control knob 2: input CC 49; ring-style CC 57; Volume style (2).\n- Track Control knob 3: input CC 50; ring-style CC 58; Pan style (3).\n- Cue Level: input CC 47; relative steps, with no ring-style output.\n\nThe first three encoder rows send each knob value back on its CC and set\nthe ring style separately. Cue Level sends relative movement rather than\nan absolute position, so its example is read-only.\n\nReferences\n\n- Akai APC40 Communications Protocol, Revision 1 — Mk1 modes, note\nmapping, and clip-launch LED velocities.\nhttps://cdn.inmusicbrands.com/akai/apc40/APC40_Communications_Protocol_r\nev_1.pdf_1db97c1fdba23bacf47df0f9bf64e913.pdf",
      "Label": "Readme - Modes and encoder feedback",
      "Color": {
        "X": 0.6,
        "Y": 0.6,
        "Z": 0.6,
        "W": 1.0
      },
      "Position": {
        "X": 1100.0,
        "Y": 34.0
      },
      "Size": {
        "X": 900.0,
        "Y": 850.0
      }
    },
